1.problem link - https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/description/


2.solution
SELECT e.name AS Employee
FROM Employee e
JOIN Employee m
ON e.managerId = m.id
WHERE e.salary > m.salary;


3.Why JOIN and not LEFT JOIN?
This is an important concept!

INNER JOIN (JOIN):
- Returns only matching records.
- Employees without managers won't appear.
- Perfect for this question because we need a manager's salary to compare.

LEFT JOIN:
- Returns all employees, even those without managers.
- Their manager-related columns would contain NULL.
- We would still need the salary comparison to filter them out.
Therefore, JOIN is sufficient and cleaner here.


4. Time and Space Complexity
Let n be the number of employees.
Complexity	Expected
Time	O(n) average with a hash join
Space	O(n) for a hash join

Note: Actual performance depends on the database execution plan, indexes, and join algorithm. SQL doesn't guarantee a particular complexity.


